/**

@page page_exahype_performance_optimisation Performance optimisation

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE performance analysis combines the runner's decomposition statistics
with a profile of the numerical kernels. @ref page_peano_performance_optimisation
describes the grid and traversal metrics.

# Data access

Generated patch data resides in rank-local flat pools. A traversal event names
the pool slots for its cell, faces, vertices, and coarse-grid context, and the
observer passes references to the action sets. Fusing consecutive numerical
operations can still improve cache reuse and reduce repeated traversal and
temporary-data traffic.

# Kernel cost

Fluxes, eigenvalues, non-conservative products, sources, and Riemann solves
should account for a substantial fraction of a compute-bound run. If they do,
apply the vectorisation and accelerator guidance on
@ref page_exahype_gpu. The CPU and the GPU evaluate the same kernel.

If memory movement or observer glue dominates, inspect patch size, temporary
arrays, callback fusion, and the number of traversals per time step before
tuning the kernel itself.

# Traversal task balance

`MPI Load Balance` describes the distribution of leaf cells. `Cells per Task`
and `Thread Ceiling` describe the subtree decomposition on each rank. The
phase report line `Utilisation` measures the realised use of those tasks.

A geometrically balanced mesh can remain computationally imbalanced when only
a few patches enter a limiter, carry many particles, or execute an expensive
material model. A solver-specific profile or work counter identifies this
case. Solver-created tasks and parallel kernels can distribute the expensive
callback work beyond its traversal task.

# Solver tasks

Few large solver tasks create a long tail after most traversal work has
finished. Increase their priority when they lie on the next time-step critical
path, or divide their numerical work when the kernel supports independent
subranges. Dividing work does not remove an algorithmic dependency that makes
the expensive task unavailable until late in the sweep.

Many small tasks create scheduling overhead. Increase task granularity or fuse
compatible patch work while preserving incoming and outgoing dependencies.
OpenMP profiles and targeted tarch instrumentation show whether ready work is
available when threads idle.

# Hybrid runs

MPI ranks exchange face and vertex state while interior subtree and solver
tasks execute. A rank with little interior work cannot hide this exchange.
Compare rank balance, spine replication, boundary waits, and solver-task load
before changing the MPI and thread layout.

Processor affinity can reduce the thread count visible to a rank. Confirm the
runtime thread report and avoid oversubscription when comparing layouts.

# Adaptive runs

Dynamic AMR can change both the solver workload and the rank ownership. The
runner reports balance and task metrics before and after each drain. Profile
the AMR phase separately from time stepping when structure construction,
migration, or repeated refinement levels dominate.

*/
